---
import SiteLayout from "../../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";

export const title = "Biking the Sunrise Road";
export const date = "2026-06-15";
export const excerpt =
  "Biking the closed road up to Sunrise at Mount Rainier — 14 miles of uninterrupted mountain, no cars, and more elevation than we expected.";
export const tags = ["biking", "mount-rainier", "washington"];
const description =
  "Biking the closed road up to Sunrise at Mount Rainier: 14 miles of uninterrupted mountain, no cars, and more elevation than we expected.";
---

<SiteLayout title={`${title} — Saachi K Gupta`} description={description}>
  <h1>{title}</h1>
  <p style="opacity:0.7; font-size: 13px;">{date}</p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The Sunrise Road at Mount Rainier reaches the highest visitors center in the park, and is only open seasonally. In 2020, I learned that it opens earlier for bikers than cars, creating a special opportunity to be in the park in (relative) solitude, and to do an iconic drive/climb without any cars! My friends Desi and Matt did the climb in 2020, and I remember being so impressed. At the time, I didn't bike, and could not ever imagine that I would voluntarily climb a mountain self-powered. 
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    My friend John mentioned he was thinking about biking Sunrise on a Friday in June of 2026 at a BBQ, and I said I'd be down to go to the park and just do the short (but very steep) hike that goes up to the Sunrise visitors center from White River Campground. I had done that hike on my last day of the Wonderland Trail in 2021, right before leaving Seattle for B-School in Philly, and it felt like it would be nice to re-visit the same path. But as I discussed this with my friend Sarah while we were swimming on Saturday at Colman Pool (incredible pool), we persuaded each other to 1) Wake up early to go to Rainier and 2) To (attempt) to bike it.
  </p>

  <img
    src="/images/sunrise-road/pre-ride-mirror.jpg"
    alt="Mirror Selfie with my road bike before picking up my friend!"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 12px; margin: 20px 0;"
  />

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
  John and I drove (in my car - so I could bail on the climb if I wanted to) to Rainier at 5:30am (!!), and parked at White River Campground. We started at the gate to the Sunrise Road, which had a sign up about recreational use being allowed while the road stayed closed to vehicles. Felt pretty smug about having this iconic road to ourselves. 
  </p>

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/sunrise-road/gate-sign-friend.jpg"
    alt="Gate sign: Recreational Use Allowed on Roadway, with friend at the bottom of the closed road"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 12px; margin: 20px 0;"
  />

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">Seven miles up</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The climb is about 2,700 feet over 12ish mile - accounts differe between Garmin, Strava, All-Trails, Maps, etc - but I subscribe to the ignorance is bliss mindset when it comes to endurance sports. The less I think about it the less I can stress about it! I had loaded up on snacks, because (for the best) I'd asked Claude what to pack while at TJ's and armed myself with four types of gummies, lots of water, and dates. The road starts with steady forest climbing and quick vistas of the Cascade range, before opening up to jaw-dropping scenery. I've been to Rainier dozens of times, and the final miles up never cease to amaze me. Getting to savor the view going VERY slowly on a bike made it even prettier.
  </p>

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/sunrise-road/rainier-straight-ahead.jpg"
    alt="Rainier straight ahead at the end of the empty closed road"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 12px; margin: 20px 0;"
  />

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/sunrise-road/handlebar-pov-rainier.jpg"
    alt="Handlebar POV riding toward Rainier on the open high-elevation road"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 12px; margin: 20px 0;"
  />

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/sunrise-road/bike-shadow-road.jpg"
    alt="Bike shadow on the dappled road through the trees"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 12px; margin: 20px 0;"
  />

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    There were sections with wildflowers (my favorite part of summer at Mt. Rainier) and incredible vista points. I had told myself that I would turn back after 5 miles if I wasn't feeling good. At five miles, I realized I had it in me. Thankfully, I had pre-downloaded music to my Tri-Headphones (so 2012!) and could listen to 2010s Alt-Pop while slowwwwwly pedaling on my lowest gera. 
  </p>

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/sunrise-road/road-wildflowers-rainier.jpg"
    alt="Road curving up with rock wall on right, wildflowers, and Rainier in the distance"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 12px; margin: 20px 0;"
  />

  <h2 style="margin-top: 28px;">Somewhere near the top</h2>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    We made it all the way to Sunrise. The snow walls that had been pushed to the side started showing up, and the awe just increased with every hairpin turn. 
  </p>

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/sunrise-road/legs-meadow-rainier.jpg"
    alt="Resting in meadow grass with Rainier close and large behind"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 12px; margin: 20px 0;"
  />

  <img loading="lazy"
    src="/images/sunrise-road/friend-bike-overhead.jpg"
    alt="Friend lifting bike overhead with Rainier right there behind"
    style="width: 100%; max-width: 480px; border-radius: 12px; margin: 20px 0;"
  />

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The descent was scary - we zoomed down for 12 straight miles, watching for road-crews. When we reached the gate, it was now closed to cyclists, as road-work had resumed. Waking up at dawn was worth it. We shotgunned Rainiers (ofc) in the parking lot, and drove back to Seatle. A perfect Seattle Sunday. (<a href="https://www.strava.com/activities/18951153929">Strava</a>)
  </p>

  <p style="max-width: 75ch;">
    The next day I drove two-plus hours to the North Cascades for Park Butte, where my brake caliper fell off my car, stranding me in the N. Cascades. I felt immense relief that the same thing had not happened 24 hours earlier zooming on i5....
  </p>
</SiteLayout>
